fact WorkItem(id: "WI-1075", description: some(value: "ABSOLUTE PATHS GET A SPELLING (`::a.b.c`), AND A BARE DOTTED PATH BECOMES PURELY RELATIVE. One spelling per meaning.\n\nTHE DEFECT (measured, WI-999). A qualified path whose HEAD is SHADOWED re-roots at the\nbare GLOBAL twin instead of resolving relatively — silently. All three rows drive the\nSAME unedited body:\n\n  namespace outer\n    namespace inner\n      operation g(x: Int64) -> Int64 = 1\n      sort Box\n        entity box(v: Int64)\n        operation use(b: Box) -> Int64 = inner.g(b.v)\n      end\n    end\n  end\n\n  base                                          => outer.inner.g\n  + a member `Box.inner`                        => LOUD 'unknown functor'\n  + `Box.inner` AND a top-level `namespace inner` => LOADS CLEAN, calls the TOP-LEVEL\n                                                    inner.g  (proved by giving the twin a\n                                                    different return type: the same file\n                                                    then fails 'expected Int64, got Bool')\n\nWHY IT CANNOT BE FIXED BY A RULE OVER THE EXISTING TEXT. The bug and a CAPABILITY are\nstructurally identical at the point of decision — head resolves locally, rung 1\n(head-qualification) misses, rung 2 (dotted_absolute) hits:\n\n  capability  outer.inner.g  with `outer` shadowed   -> rung 2 supplies the right answer\n  bug         inner.g        with `inner` shadowed   -> rung 2 supplies a foreign answer\n\nThe only difference is whether the author meant the path absolutely, which is not in the\ntext — a relative path can COINCIDE with some other symbol's fully-qualified name. So\nevery rule keyed on the current syntax picks one side and loses the other. Measured:\nmaking rung 2 stand down whenever the head resolved (`head_owns_path -> head_sym.is_some()`)\nfails 8 tests, 4 of them wi751's capability.\n\nTHE CAPABILITY IS REAL AND MUST SURVIVE. Measured: an FQN needs no import and is immune to\nshadowing today, including of its OUTERMOST segment —\n\n  sort Box\n    operation outer(n: Int64) -> Bool = true\n    operation inner(n: Int64) -> Bool = true\n    operation use(b: Box) -> Int64 = outer.inner.g(b.v)   -- loads clean, returns Int64\n  end\n\nwith the relative spelling `inner.g(b.v)` in the same fixture answering 'unknown functor'.\n\nTHE DESIGN (user decision, 2026-08-11).\n\n  a.b.c     Resolved THROUGH THE SCOPE CHAIN: bind the head at scope-distance 0, then\n            resolve the rest under that binding. A miss under the head is LOUD; the path\n            is never re-anchored somewhere else.\n\n            THIS STILL REACHES THE ROOT, and that is why the migration is zero rather\n            than lucky. The scope walk goes out to _global, where a top-level namespace\n            is an ordinary local — so with nothing shadowing `outer`, the head of\n            `outer.inner.g` binds to the top-level `outer` and the path resolves exactly\n            as today. MEASURED: the unshadowed FQN loads with the rung-2 instrument\n            silent, i.e. rung 1 carried it. `::` is needed ONLY where something shadows\n            the head.\n\n  ::a.b.c   ABSOLUTE, always. Straight to by_qualified_name — the same channel `import`\n            already uses (process_imports resolves its path with a bare\n            by_qualified_name.get, no scope walk), so nothing can shadow it.\n\nWHAT ACTUALLY CHANGES — one row of three:\n\n  nothing shadows the head                     unchanged (rung 1)\n  head shadowed, path resolves under it        unchanged (rung 1)\n  head shadowed, path MISSES under it          today: rung 2 silently re-anchors the\n                                               whole path at a global twin.\n                                               proposed: loud, and `::a.b.c` is how you\n                                               ask for the root.\n\nTO RE-ROOT, in this ticket's vocabulary, is to throw away the scope reading after it\nmisses and reinterpret the literal path text as a top-level qualified name — re-anchoring\nwhat the path is relative to. That is step 3 of: (1) head `inner` binds to `Box.inner`,\n(2) no `g` under it, (3) reinterpret \"inner.g\" as a top-level name and find an unrelated\none. Only step 3 is removed.\n\nNON-COEXISTING, and that is the point. If `::` were additive while an unmarked path stayed\nabsolute-when-it-needs-to-be, there would be two spellings for one meaning differing only\nin a rare corner — and the safe one is the one nobody writes, because the unmarked one\nappears to work. That is the defect 059 refuses for `fact Spec[X]` vs `provides Spec[X]`.\nSo the marker REPLACES the implicit absolute reading; it is not offered beside it.\n\nWHY `::` AND NOT A NAME. Verified free: `::` appears in tree-sitter-anthill/grammar.js only\ninside comments, and the pratt operator table is ! != % & * + - -> / : < <= <=> = === > >=\n@ ^ | — no `::`. It burns NO identifier, so the marker itself cannot be shadowed. `_root_`\n(Scala) and `_global` (which IS the root scope's owner symbol — global_scope() interns that\nexact string) both name something real but are ordinary identifiers, so a declaration can\nshadow the escape hatch itself, and reserving them cuts into the `_`-prefix space intern.rs\nleaves to users.\n\nWHAT OTHER LANGUAGES ANSWER, measured on this machine (Rust edition 2021, Scala 3.8.2,\njavac 21), same fixture in each:\n\n  Rust    mod lib + fn lib COEXIST; lib::f(1) reaches the module, lib(1) the function.\n          Separate TYPE / VALUE / MACRO namespaces, so a value declaration can never\n          capture a path prefix. mod lib + struct lib is refused AT THE DECLARATION:\n          'lib must be defined only once in the type namespace'. Not available to Anthill,\n          which merges every category onto one symbol (WI-926).\n  Scala 3 shadows; SILENT when the shadowing value's type happens to have the same member\n          chain (2 => 1001). Escape is _root_.\n  Java    shadows; SILENT identically (2 => 1001). JLS 6.4.2 obscuring, precedence\n          variable > type > package. NO escape; the mitigation is the naming convention\n          and 'import the type'.\n\nAnthill today is worse in KIND than Scala/Java: their silent answer at least comes from the\nobject the author wrote as `lib`; ours comes from an unrelated top-level namespace nobody\nnamed in that path.\n\nSCOPE. One token, one grammar rule, and one arm in resolve_dotted_in_kb — the ladder is\nshared by six resolvers (WI-752) so it lands in one place and every position inherits it\n(term, type, proof/describe citation; kernel-language.md 8.6 says the ladder is\nposition-independent). Retiring the implicit reading is the same function.\n\nMIGRATION: ZERO, MEASURED. An instrumented dotted_absolute (logging every hit, with the\nhead's resolved state) over stdlib + anthill-stl + github-todo + sql-store + webots +\nring-polynom + anthill-todo records ZERO rung-2 hits. Instrument self-checked: it fires on\nboth the capability shape and the bug shape, so the zero is coverage and not silence.\n\nDO NOT BREAK THE VISIBILITY FALL-THROUGH. WI-752 deliberately made a head-qualified hit\nhidden by `internal` keep descending — 'a rung's hit being unusable is a reason to try the\nNEXT rung'. A head whose hit is REJECTED FOR VISIBILITY has not bound the path, so the\ndescent must continue there. The naive one-line change conflates the two and fails\nwi752_internal_head_hit_falls_through_to_the_absolute_rung and three of its neighbours;\nthose four are a separate correction, not part of the capability change.\n\nRENAME `head_owns_path` when its condition changes — the name states the old rule.\n\nTESTS TO REWRITE (4, and they are the deliberate change, not collateral):\nwi751_root_shadowing_declaration_keeps_qualified_paths (its acceptance states the OLD\nprinciple in as many words — 'an inert declaration cannot change what a fully-qualified\npath means' — which becomes true only for the `::` spelling),\nwi751_absolute_path_respects_internal_visibility,\nwi751_entity_field_does_not_capture_a_namespace_path,\nwi751_rule_reference_citation_under_a_shadowed_root. Each keeps its fixture and gains\n`::` on the path.\n\nRELATION TO WI-999. 059 R4 clause 3 declines to refuse a declaration that captures a\nNAMESPACE name, on the ground that a namespace has no answer for a body to get a different\none of. Row 3 above refutes that literally — the answer that changes is a qualified path's —\nso the exclusion currently rests on the hazard being rare rather than absent. This ticket\nmakes it absent: with `::` the absolute reading is spelled and the relative one is loud, so\ncapturing a namespace name can neither re-root a path nor silently change one.\n\nACCEPTANCE: drive all three rows of the defect and assert the VALUE each answers, not that\nthey load; keep the differing-return-type variant as the control that proves WHICH `g` was\nbound. Drive `::a.b.c` resolving with its head shadowed, and the unmarked twin being loud.\nDrive the head_owns_path control — a genuine member miss under a namespace root stays loud —\nand the wi752 internal fall-through. Say at each test's site which rows fail when the change\nis backed out. Re-run the rung-2 census and assert it stays zero. Run rustland/scripts/test.sh."), context: none, acceptance: [ToolPasses(tool: "cargo-test", params: none)], depends_on: some(value: nil), generates: none, requires_capability: none, status: Delivered(agent: "user", at: "2026-08-11T12:08:00Z"))
